WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.859
In this demonstration we investigate a web server's access log, step by step.

2
00:00:05.859 --> 00:00:12.170
The log comes from a fictitious LGU e-services portal where citizens check their permits.

3
00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:18.055
It is a different log from the one in your activity, so the method is what matters.

4
00:00:18.055 --> 00:00:23.727
Our goal: find the suspicious requests, and decide which one is the most urgent.

5
00:00:24.827 --> 00:00:27.099
First, how big is the log?

6
00:00:27.099 --> 00:00:30.856
wc -l counts the lines: seventeen requests.

7
00:00:30.856 --> 00:00:39.155
Small enough to read, but let's be systematic anyway, because real logs have millions of lines.

8
00:00:40.255 --> 00:00:43.256
Next, the first three lines with head.

9
00:00:43.256 --> 00:00:52.019
These are normal: a citizen's PC loading the home page and a script file, and a phone checking a permit status.

10
00:00:52.019 --> 00:00:55.966
All got status 200, success, from normal browsers.

11
00:00:55.966 --> 00:01:03.940
Remember the fields: client IP, timestamp, request, status code, size, and at the end the user agent,

12
00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:06.703
the software that made the request.

13
00:01:07.804 --> 00:01:09.590
Now the whole file.

14
00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:12.504
Don't read it line by line yet.

15
00:01:12.504 --> 00:01:15.419
Let the computer count for you.

16
00:01:15.419 --> 00:01:21.153
This awk command prints only the first field, the IP address.

17
00:01:21.153 --> 00:01:29.145
Then sort, uniq dash C counts each one, and sort dash R N puts the busiest IP on top.

18
00:01:29.145 --> 00:01:36.195
One address stands out: 198.51.100.140, with eight requests in a small log.

19
00:01:37.295 --> 00:01:39.621
Let's see what that IP did.

20
00:01:39.621 --> 00:01:41.860
grep shows only its lines.

21
00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:49.783
Six POST requests to the admin login page in six seconds, all with status 401, unauthorized.

22
00:01:49.783 --> 00:01:52.538
Wrong password, again and again.

23
00:01:52.538 --> 00:01:57.878
And the user agent is python-requests, a script, not a person.

24
00:01:57.878 --> 00:02:02.184
That is a password-guessing attack, a brute force.

25
00:02:03.283 --> 00:02:05.798
Now the most important two lines.

26
00:02:05.798 --> 00:02:14.559
On the seventh try the status changes to 302, a redirect, which is what a login page does after a successful login.

27
00:02:14.559 --> 00:02:18.673
And then a GET of the admin dashboard with status 200.

28
00:02:18.673 --> 00:02:20.197
The attacker got in.

29
00:02:20.197 --> 00:02:27.740
A successful attack is always more urgent than a blocked one, so this goes to the top of my report.

30
00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.263
Next, the user agents.

31
00:02:31.263 --> 00:02:33.448
zgrab is a scanning tool.

32
00:02:33.448 --> 00:02:40.267
This IP asked for a WordPress login page, a git config file and a backup file.

33
00:02:40.267 --> 00:02:45.774
Status 404 every time: not found, these files don't exist here.

34
00:02:45.774 --> 00:02:49.183
That is someone probing for weaknesses.

35
00:02:49.183 --> 00:02:54.515
It failed, so it's lower priority, but I still record the IP.

36
00:02:55.616 --> 00:02:56.998
One more pattern.

37
00:02:56.998 --> 00:03:03.340
This search request contains a script tag, an attempt at cross-site scripting,

38
00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:07.487
where an attacker tries to inject code into a page.

39
00:03:07.487 --> 00:03:09.845
The status is 403, forbidden.

40
00:03:09.845 --> 00:03:13.098
The web application firewall blocked it.

41
00:03:13.098 --> 00:03:15.943
Blocked, but still worth recording.

42
00:03:17.044 --> 00:03:19.700
Here is my finished findings table.

43
00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:26.911
For each suspicious IP: what was attempted, the status, whether it succeeded, and the priority.

44
00:03:26.911 --> 00:03:31.466
Brute force with a successful login, critical, escalate now.

45
00:03:31.466 --> 00:03:38.373
The scanner and the cross-site scripting attempt failed, so they are lower, but documented.

46
00:03:38.373 --> 00:03:42.699
This is the same table you will fill in for Activity 2.3.

47
00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:44.593
Your turn.

48
00:03:44.593 --> 00:03:48.083
In the CTM Virtual Lab, choose Activity 2.3.

49
00:03:48.083 --> 00:04:01.252
On your own Ubuntu VM, investigate web underscore access dot log in the assets folder with the same steps: count by IP, follow the status codes, read the user agents.

50
00:04:01.252 --> 00:04:09.264
Record each suspicious IP, what was attempted, the status, and whether it succeeded in the worksheet,

51
00:04:09.264 --> 00:04:11.485
then press Check my answers.

52
00:04:11.485 --> 00:04:14.975
Flag anything that succeeded as most urgent.

